# 098-engine-surface.syms — what code arriving after the build may call.
#
# What this is: the list of engine symbols kept in the executable's
# dynamic symbol table. A box or a map compiled while the program runs
# arrives as a shared object and is opened with dlopen; a shared object
# can only call a symbol the host executable exported. This file is
# that export list, and so it is the engine's public surface written
# down — the first time that boundary has existed anywhere except by
# accident.
#
# How it does it, in general terms: the linker is handed this file as
# --dynamic-list. Every name matching a pattern here goes into the
# dynamic table and becomes a root the section collector has to keep.
# Everything else is fair game for --gc-sections to discard.
#
# Why this is not -rdynamic (issue 311d). -rdynamic exports *every*
# global symbol, and an exported symbol is a collection root by
# definition: the whole point of exporting it is that code which does
# not exist yet may look it up by name, so the linker can prove nothing
# about who calls it and must keep it. -rdynamic therefore declares the
# entire binary reachable, and --gc-sections then collects nothing. The
# two flags are in direct opposition and -rdynamic wins.
#
# Measured on a five-function model before this file was written: a
# program placing one box out of three kept one shim under
# --gc-sections alone, and all three shims plus all three station
# builders once -rdynamic was added. With this file it keeps one and
# still publishes the engine.
#
# And on a real test binary, publishing everything costs 126,225 bytes
# of code against 117,400 for the list below — the difference being
# engine internals nothing reaches.
#
# What the linker sheds is the engine's own unreached internals. It
# does not shed the boxes, and that is decided below rather than by any
# flag: publishing a station-builder is what lets code compiled later
# bind to a box compiled now, and a published symbol is one the linker
# may never discard.
#
# The reachability it computes is exact — through hand-written extern
# declarations and through function pointers taken by name, the two
# cases a source parser gets wrong.
#
# A symbol listed here survives collection even when nothing compiled
# into the binary calls it, which is the property the whole idea rests
# on: a box arriving mid-run calls engine functions that this
# particular program may never call itself.
#
# Why prefixes rather than a list of names. Naming families says which
# layers are public without a list that has to be re-edited every time
# one of them gains a function. The narrower alternative — naming the
# individual functions — was considered and refused, because the set is
# not "what generated code calls" but "what code compiled later may
# bind to", and that includes one symbol per box. Such a list would
# have to be regenerated by the build, which makes it a derived
# artifact rather than a statement somebody wrote and can read.
#
# Leaving a family out is not a silent loss: a shared object that needs
# a symbol the host did not export fails at dlopen, naming the symbol.

{
    # The construction surface. A station-builder calls into it to
    # write a station; a compiled map calls it to build a whole
    # program; the construction boxes call it on behalf of a map that
    # is building another map.
    map_*;

    # Two things share this prefix, and both belong here.
    #
    # Stopping — a box may end the program it is running inside, which
    # is the one thing a box does that is not pure computation.
    #
    # And every generated station-builder, whose symbol carries the
    # box's full path under this prefix. Those are published for a
    # different reason than everything else in this file: not because
    # the engine calls them, but because **a map compiled next year has
    # to be able to bind to a box compiled today**. A shared object can
    # only bind to a name the program it was loaded into published, so
    # a program that publishes no station-builders can only be extended
    # by boxes carrying their own copies of everything they touch.
    #
    # This is where the cost noted at the top actually lands. A
    # published station-builder is a root, and it holds its shim and
    # the box body behind it, so a program keeps every box it was built
    # with: 117,400 bytes of code on the binary measured above, against
    # 95,742 if these were left unpublished.
    #
    # Extendable at run time is a requirement of this engine; small was
    # never one, and a program extendable only by boxes it already
    # contained would be extendable in name only.
    sora_*;
};
